{:edb/db-before-t 2471 :edb/db-after-t 2472 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535784 50 #inst "2026-10-09T07:09:53.874Z" 13194139535784 true] [17592186152113 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366186 13194139535784 true] [17592186152114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366179 13194139535784 true] [17592186152115 1020 #edb/ref 17592186366172 13194139535784 true] [17592186152717 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" 13194139535784 false] [17592186152717 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366172 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366173 13194139535784 true] [17592186366172 1089 "q-79273" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535784 true] [17592186366172 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/48c5190fc4f45f4bf3e76a56977339559ceb30ca90a41411d3bc4df2362b0787.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366172 1113 #uuid "b9d06d5a-1710-506f-9441-28c4dc1ef4cf" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366172 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/546b9f72124a7eb05877db3600e40b5772404a1a3378de67cfbe3320c78ac2ba.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366174 13194139535784 true] [17592186366173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366175 13194139535784 true] [17592186366173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366176 13194139535784 true] [17592186366173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366177 13194139535784 true] [17592186366173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366178 13194139535784 true] [17592186366173 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535784 true] [17592186366173 1041 "selection" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366173 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366175 13194139535784 true] [17592186366173 1110 #uuid "3990cf93-b107-596e-989a-e69158aa996d" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366174 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535784 true] [17592186366174 1111 #uuid "d083840a-941f-529b-92bc-a21417ee64af" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366175 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535784 true] [17592186366175 1111 #uuid "68d6142e-0b01-5ece-8ab8-4e0cd772b887" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366176 1034 "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535784 true] [17592186366176 1111 #uuid "348d0679-80cc-51a3-844c-3795fddf01c2" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366177 1034 "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535784 true] [17592186366177 1111 #uuid "46b9267c-93a3-503b-a07f-904d0aa1048a" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366178 1034 "\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535784 true] [17592186366178 1111 #uuid "4cb54a88-8eb5-5849-a2ec-91249798d06c" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366179 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366180 13194139535784 true] [17592186366179 1089 "q-79287" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535784 true] [17592186366179 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070244/assets/07ec13485a828c8f5cd99ae2fbc3f7444135ba3c1cdf790762edb01b9726317e.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366179 1113 #uuid "9082f86b-7c0f-5dd7-b074-5c0e7d067128" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366179 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,2),$ $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366181 13194139535784 true] [17592186366180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366182 13194139535784 true] [17592186366180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366183 13194139535784 true] [17592186366180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366184 13194139535784 true] [17592186366180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366185 13194139535784 true] [17592186366180 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535784 true] [17592186366180 1041 "selection" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366180 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366182 13194139535784 true] [17592186366180 1110 #uuid "f735d59c-2e8a-5e2f-bfbe-439b61b0d80d" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366181 1034 "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535784 true] [17592186366181 1111 #uuid "63d32c88-76e2-5390-82c6-9eb900a34c1a" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366182 1034 "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366182 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535784 true] [17592186366182 1111 #uuid "9c04c4a0-2411-5cd3-a749-0d949b6aed42" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366183 1034 "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535784 true] [17592186366183 1111 #uuid "0d3a4619-1ca2-528b-920a-f4767aa1db0b" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366184 1034 "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535784 true] [17592186366184 1111 #uuid "7a077b25-808c-5e1f-8296-4b190dd6f6db" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366185 1034 "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535784 true] [17592186366185 1111 #uuid "13435a9e-8fec-5dbb-add7-b89405f650a5" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366186 1017 #edb/ref 17592186366187 13194139535784 true] [17592186366186 1089 "q-79377" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366186 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535784 true] [17592186366186 1100 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366186 1113 #uuid "7905e0ad-e4b9-544c-af54-d96f3b36bc86" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366186 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{x+5}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2 \\\\ \\frac{y+2}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1.\n$$" 13194139535784 true] [17592186366187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366188 13194139535784 true] [17592186366187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366189 13194139535784 true] [17592186366187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366190 13194139535784 true] [17592186366187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366191 13194139535784 true] [17592186366187